Geoff Dale, AnarchyPPL - Anarch (Adjudicator), comments:

> My comments on the various PPL proposals floating around.
> 
> 1> Mike Price's InterPPL negotiation framework.
> 
> Simon! replies to Mike Price:
>>> My own proposal for the inter-PPL charter is simplicity itself.
>>> Anyone can file charges against anyone, but they are judged solely
>>> against the defendant's PPL charter or etiquette code.  A natural
>>> Schelling point I feel, although more complex solutions must be
>>> possible.
>>
>> One last time: I agree.
> 
> I'm not sure I do, entirely.

[problems with pressing cross PPL charges detailed]

Your criticisms are valid.  Here's my modified proposal.

Charges between two people in different PPLs are judged by the 
heads of both respective PPLs to ensure that the charges are 
compatible with both PPLs' charters.  This makes the charges that
people can file against each other, even in different PPLs, 
symmetrical and equal.  Also offsets the greater posting freedoms 
of a more liberal PPL with greater charge filing restrictions.

For instance, Bob in PPL-A could not bring charges against Alice 
in PPL1 for "setting the record straight".  Whereas Bob could bring
charges against Alice in PPL1 (or PPL-A, for that matter) for
"debating the basics".  Alice in PPL1 could bring exactly the same
debating the basics charges against Bob.  Whilst Alice can't set the
record straight (without being charged) she can file charges against
others for trying to set the record straight, which Bob can't do at
all.  Bob, OTOH, can set the record straight without being punished.

> In my PPL's case:
> If a slanderous/libelous act were committed against one of my clients
> by a member of another PPL (and my client wished to "press charges"),
> I would feel obligated to follow up on it. If this action were not
> "illegal" in the other PPL, then the inter-PPL negotiation would begin
> in earnest.

I sincerely and earnestly hope that ExI would never approve a PPL in
which slander/libel was permitted.

> On the other hand:
> Alice, a member of PPL-1 flames Bob, who is a member of PPL-A. Can he
> press charges? If he does, who should he contact, Simon! or myself?

Under my modified proposal Alice would contact Simon! who would check
that the charges fall within the letter of PPL1's law.  If acceptable
to PPL1's charter Simon! passes the charges to Geoff, who checks that
such charges are compatible with PPL-A's charter.  If not acceptable at
either stage they are rejected.  If acceptable PPL-A then follows it's
own charter processing the charge against Bob just as if another PPL-A
client had bought them.

> The answer is: Its a free market, try it and find out what works.

I agree, it's a free market.  I'm suggesting that we think about how it
might work before any fireworks start.

> I probably wouldn't launch into an inter-PPL negotiation to prosecute
> something that wasn't illegal, but if Bob flamed back and Alice
> pressed charges, we obviously have a problem.

No more than we do in a "real-life" PPL situation.  PPL clients have to
get used to the idea that relations between clients in different PPLs
are more restricted or formalised than relations between clients in the
same PPL.

> But, I do agree with the basic principle. If an act is illegal in both
> PPLs, then the interPPL negotiation is a no-brainer. I'm not sure how
> a truly free market would handle rogue PPLs that protect their members
> from anything no matter how atrocious said members act.

In real life the other PPLs would probably gang up and coerce a widely
perceived "atrocious" PPL into taking it's business elsewhere or
exterminate them.  As Perry would no doubt explain if he were active. 
On the List, if such a rogue PPL were spawned then I'm ExI would step
in and revoke the PPL's charter.  Such an act would no doubt cause a lot
of heat and discussion, which is why I'm sure each PPL's charter will
be vetted most carefully on inception by ExI.

> There is also the "outlaw" system that Ray proposed.

If you're referring to PPL2 (?), I proposed, abandoned it and joined 
PPL-A :-)

