Subject: [#94-1-632] PHIL/POL/TECH: nanarchy

  EXTROPY 11  features an account of nanarchy in the ongoing Mark S.  Miller
interview.   I  read  this account with  great interest, although I found it
intuitively disturbing.  I think I found it bothersome for these reasons:

  Should  we force  others to behave?  If any aggressive act were  to be met
with  sure and swift retribution, it is unlikely one would even choose to be
an aggressor (e.g., if  we  guarantee the wages of sin  are death.)  Doesn't
freedom, of necessity, entail the possibility of being a monster?  Should we
remove any  practical  choice of being  evil?  (However we  choose to define
"evil.")

  If we have the means to compel  others to adhere to libertarian notions of
right  and wrong, don't the means  exist to compel adherance to _any_ notion
of right  and wrong?  (Also,  It would  be interesting to examine tech which
could distinguish  between  actual  and  apparent aggression.  For  example,
consensual domination/submission activities.)


  Would nanarchy  be more morally palatable if it  were instituted as a PPL?
One would theoretically have the out of leaving the PPL.  Also, an nanarchic
PPL would  likely  give outside  aggressors  pause  (arbitration  mechanisms
still being needed for inter-PPL disputes).

Thoughts?

