From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 94 10:41:11 EST
Subject: [#94-1-636] LEGAL:  Proposal to encourage PPL participation

In #94-1-618 Mike Price says:
>I would rather we gave the PPL system a chance to work first.

I have seen this desire expressed often in recent weeks.  The PPL system 
has been available for a few years.  Its problem thus far is that everyone 
is placed in a default PPL and almost everyone stays there. If almost 
everyone is in the same PPL, it is not a valid test of poly-centric law.

I propose the following steps to solve the one-PPL problem:

1.  Send a very brief description of the PPL system along with a List of 
existing PPLs to each New List member.  Have them choose their PPL instead 
of lumping everyone one into a default PPL.  (I suggest this be a brief 
e-mail query upon joining, not contained within the rather lengthy Welcome 
message.)

2.  For existing members, treat them as above, making everyone respond by 
choosing a PPL.  Any nonresponses can be placed in existing PPLs randomly 
in an even distribution.  (Persons not in PPL-1 have already answered this 
query.  Only people in PPL-1 need to be polled to determine who has chosen 
PPL-1 and who is in it by default.)

3.  Optional:  Create an Outlaw status for people not wanting to be 
protected by a PPL, or people who do not respond to the PPL query.  Such 
people can have judgements brought against them via any PPL, and have no 
way to defend themselves.  Such a person can have any judgements brought 
against them, but can never bring charges against other individuals, nor 
can they defend themselves.
